<TopicPage slug="subjects/medicine/expert/genomic-medicine">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Genomic medicine uses <strong>a patient's DNA as clinical data</strong> — for
    diagnosis, drug selection, risk prediction and family planning. The Human Genome
    Project's promise is arriving unevenly: spectacular in cancer pharmacogenomics and
    rare diseases, still maturing for common complex diseases — with interpretation
    and ethics as the bottlenecks, not the sequencing. The syllabus hands the topic
    two anchors — the reproductive system (27) and development and inheritance (28) —
    and they are the right anchors: genomic medicine is, at bottom, the clinical
    consequences of the transmission and expression of inherited information. Every
    section below reads a stage of that pipeline — gametes to zygote to genotype to
    drug response — as a clinical instrument.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four clinical uses and their open problems" widget="equationStep" config={genomeCfg} />

  <h2>The reproductive system as an information channel</h2>
  <p>
    The reproductive system (27) is where genomes are packaged and shuffled. Meiosis
    in the gonads makes gametes, and its two mechanisms — independent assortment and
    crossing over (recombination) — ensure each gamete carries a unique mosaic of the
    grandparents' chromosomes; the clinical consequences are quantitative: each
    pregnancy is an independent draw, recurrence risks for Mendelian disease are
    exact (25% for a recessive gene from two carrier parents, 50% for a dominant
    one), and maternal age effect is mechanical — ova rest in meiosis from before
    birth, so nondisjunction risk accumulates (Down syndrome's trisomy 21 risk
    rising from about 1 in 1,250 at age 25 to about 1 in 100 at 40). The clinical
    pipeline rides the same channel: preimplantation genetic testing biopsies
    embryos from IVF; non-invasive prenatal testing sequences cell-free foetal DNA
    circulating in maternal blood (from about 10 weeks, since 2011 progressively
    replacing riskier amniocentesis for aneuploidy screening); carrier screening
    panels find recessive pairs before conception. Inheritance pattern completes
    the channel: autosomal dominant, recessive, X-linked — and the mitochondrial
    exception, where mitochondria's own DNA passes maternally (mitochondrial
    replacement therapy, "three-parent IVF", exists legally in the UK for this).
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a positive non-invasive prenatal test means the
    foetus has the condition." NIPT is a <em>screening</em> test: it reads
    cell-free DNA, much of which comes from the placenta, so confined placental
    mosaicism (and vanishing twins, and maternal conditions) can produce positive
    results for a chromosomally normal foetus. For trisomy 21 the test's
    sensitivity is excellent, but the predictive value depends on the condition's
    rarity — a positive screen still requires confirmation by diagnostic testing
    (amniocentesis or CVS) before any clinical decision. Screening-high,
    confirm-what-matters is the pipeline's whole logic, and collapsing it is
    where prenatal-genomics errors live.
  </p>

  <h2>Development and inheritance: from genotype to phenotype</h2>
  <p>
    Development and inheritance (28) supply the reading rules the clinic applies.
    Genotype is not destiny by three mechanisms the clinic must navigate.
    <strong>Penetrance and expressivity</strong>: the same BRCA1 variant gives very
    different cancer risks across carriers (and the residual risk depends on the
    rest of the genome and life history) — variant reports are probabilities, not
    sentences. <strong>Epigenetics</strong>: methylation marks and chromatin state
    silence or expose genes, differ by parent of origin (imprinting — Prader–Willi
    and Angelman syndromes are the same chromosomal region with opposite outcomes
    depending on which parent it came from), and respond to environment — the
    agouti-mouse experiments showing diet methylating a gene and changing coat
    colour and obesity are the textbook demonstration. <strong>Complex
    inheritance</strong>: most common disease (diabetes, heart disease,
    schizophrenia) is polygenic — hundreds of variants each adding a sliver, now
    summed into <strong>polygenic risk scores</strong>, which predict useful slices
    of risk but carry the field's sharpest equity problem (built on
    European-ancestry data, they misperform in other populations). Twin studies
    gave the field its pre-genomic backbone (heritability estimates); genome-wide
    association studies gave it the variant lists; the clinic is where the two
    must combine into usable, honest risk statements.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "having 'the gene for' a disease means you will
    get it." Genes confer risks with specific architectures: Huntington's is
    near-fully penetrant (carriers of the expanded repeat develop it), BRCA1
    variants confer elevated lifetime risk (on the order of 55–70% for breast
    cancer, modifiable by screening and surgery), while an "Alzheimer's gene"
    (APOE4) shifts odds without determining outcome. The clinical act is
    translating a variant into a quantified, actionable probability — and much
    of genomic medicine's hard craft is exactly that translation, including the
    discipline to say "we don't know yet".
  </p>

  <h2>Pharmacogenomics: dose by DNA</h2>
  <p>
    The most mature clinical use is drug response: genes controlling drug metabolism
    decide whether a medication works, does nothing, or harms. The cytochrome P450
    enzymes are the machinery — CYP2D6, CYP2C19, CYP2C9 — and their variants sort
    patients into poor, intermediate, normal and ultra-rapid metabolisers. Worked
    case: clopidogrel, the anti-platelet drug, is a prodrug activated by CYP2C19 —
    poor metabolisers (loss-of-function alleles reach a fifth or more of some East
    Asian populations) activate little of it and suffer stent thromboses on standard
    dosing; guidelines now direct them to alternatives (ticagrelor, prasugrel), and
    the FDA's boxed warning (2010) made the test's logic official. Codeine is the
    mirror case: activated by CYP2D6, so ultra-rapid metabolisers (including
    breastfeeding mothers) can overdose — fatalities led to codeine's withdrawal
    from paediatric use in many countries. Warfarin dosing (CYP2C9 and VKORC1),
    abacavir hypersensitivity (the HLA-B*57:01 test — mandatory in much of the
    world before prescription), and thiopurine methyltransferase testing before
    mercaptopurine round out the implemented list: pharmacogenomics is genomic
    medicine's quiet daily work, one prescription at a time.
  </p>

  <h2>Cancer genomics: treating the tumour's genome, not the organ</h2>
  <p>
    Oncology is where genomic medicine changed fastest, because the tumour's
    genome <em>is</em> the disease. Sequencing a tumour finds its driver mutations,
    and targeted drugs exist for many: EGFR inhibitors for EGFR-mutant lung cancer,
    BRAF inhibitors for BRAF-V600E melanoma, HER2 antibodies (trastuzumab, since
    1998) for HER2-amplified breast cancer, imatinib (2001) for chronic myeloid
    leukaemia — the BCR-ABL fusion protein's inhibitor, the paradigm case of a
    rationally designed targeted drug (CML five-year survival transformed).
    Genomic markers also steer immunotherapy: mismatch-repair deficiency and high
    tumour mutational burden predict checkpoint-inhibitor response — and, in 2017,
    the first tissue-agnostic approval (pembrolizumab for any MSI-high cancer,
    regardless of organ). Resistance is the genomic lesson's recursive edge: tumours
    evolve under drug pressure, so resistance mutations (EGFR T790M and successors)
    demand re-biopsy and second-generation drugs — treatment as an iterative
    sequencing loop. Liquid biopsy (circulating tumour DNA) is extending the loop
    to monitoring without needles in tumours.
  </p>

  <h2>Rare disease, diagnosis and the odyssey problem</h2>
  <p>
    Rare diseases (thousands of them, collectively affecting roughly one person in
    twenty) were genomics' first clinical triumph because the mathematics favour
    it: an individual rare disease is hopeless to diagnose by pattern recognition,
    but sequencing the exome (the 1–2% of the genome that codes proteins) tests
    thousands of Mendelian candidates at once. Diagnostic yield in suspected
    Mendelian disease runs in the 25–40% range at expert centres — and each
    diagnosis ends a "diagnostic odyssey" that often lasted five to ten years of
    specialist visits, sometimes enabling targeted treatment (enzyme replacement,
    diet, or — increasingly — a gene therapy). The flip side is the field's
    central bottleneck: <strong>variants of uncertain significance</strong> — missense
    changes whose effect is unknown — dominate novel findings (a large share of
    clinical exome results), and reclassification is a standing task (CLINVAR-style
    databases, data sharing across centres, families linked by matching variants).
    Newborn sequencing expands the question forward: which childhood-onset
    treatable conditions to screen (traditional heel-prick panels cover dozens;
    genome-based screening raises storage, consent and false-positive dilemmas
    for a generation).
  </p>
  <p>
    The infrastructure response to the odyssey problem has itself become a
    template. The UK's 100,000 Genomes Project (2013–2018) embedded whole-genome
    sequencing in a national health service for rare disease and cancer, returning
    diagnoses to a meaningful fraction of participants who had waited years and
    seeding the NHS Genomic Medicine Service — the first health system to offer
    genomic sequencing routinely. Diagnostic yield rose further with <strong>matchmaking
    databases</strong>: patients worldwide carrying variants in the same gene are
    matched, and a single second case converts an uncertain finding into a
    diagnosis (the Matchmaker Exchange federation). The lesson generalises:
    rare-disease genomics is a network good — its value grows with the number of
    connected patients and centres, which is why the pipeline's most important
    artefacts are shared, versioned, cross-border data resources rather than any
    single lab's results.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "sequencing is the bottleneck — once genomes are
    cheap and fast, genomic medicine delivers itself." Sequencing is now the cheap
    part (a genome at a few hundred dollars, in days); interpretation is the
    expensive part: variant curation, functional studies, clinical correlation,
    counselling time, and the majority of rare findings remaining uninterpretable.
    The bottleneck's shape has inverted since 2003 — data outpaced knowledge —
    which is why the field's growth is now in curation infrastructure and
    genetic-counselling workforce rather than sequencers.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Gene therapy's arrival.</strong> The promise was three decades in
    landing: early trials' setbacks (the 1999 death of Jesse Gelsinger; leukaemia
    cases from insertional mutagenesis in 2000s gene-addition trials) forced the
    vector engineering that made modern therapy safe. The current generation:
    AAV-delivered gene replacement (Luxturna for inherited retinal dystrophy,
    2017; Zolgensma for spinal muscular atrophy, 2019 — a single infusion for a
    disease that was a leading infant genetic killer); ex-vivo stem-cell editing;
    and CRISPR therapeutics — Casgevy (approved 2023 in the UK and US), editing
    patients' haematopoietic stem cells to reactivate foetal haemoglobin for sickle
    cell disease and beta-thalassaemia. The frontier problems are economic (prices
    in the millions per patient — how does an insurance market amortise a
    one-time cure?) and biological (AAV cargo limits, immune responses, and the
    liver-dominated delivery tropism). The sickle-cell case is the ethics
    laboratory too: a disease of millions in sub-Saharan Africa, cured by a
    therapy costing more than most patients' lifetime earnings — genomic
    medicine's distribution problem in one line.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Population genomics and the equity problem.</strong> The reference
    resources skew: of genomes in large research databases, the overwhelming
    majority are from European-ancestry participants — with consequences at every
    layer (polygenic scores misperform; pharmacogenomic frequencies are
    ancestry-dependent; rare-disease diagnosis is harder where the reference
    panel is thin). The corrections are underway: H3Africa, the All of Us
    programme (targeting a million, deliberately diverse), UK Biobank (500,000,
    linked to health records), and Indigenous-data-sovereignty frameworks
    (governance, not just sampling). The clinical stakes are concrete: a
    pharmacogenomic panel calibrated on one population mis-doses another;
    carrier screening tuned to one population's founder mutations misses
    another's. Genomic medicine inherits genomics' sampling history, and
    fixing it is a design requirement, not an aspiration.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The ethics stack, in standing questions.</strong> Genomic data are
    family data: sequencing one person reveals relatives' risks (and identities —
    genealogy databases have solved cold cases, the Golden State Killer's 2018
    arrest being the watershed), and consent frameworks (family veto? duty to
    warn?) remain unsettled. Incidental findings force pre-emptive choices (the
    ACMG's list of genes reported regardless of the question asked — adult-onset
    risks surfacing in children's exomes). Insurance and employment protections
    (GINA in the US covers health insurance but not life or disability insurance)
    are jurisdictional patchworks. Germline editing is banned for reproduction in
    most jurisdictions after the 2018 CRISPR-babies case, while somatic therapy is
    approving its way through regulators — the line between therapy and
    enhancement is the live policy argument. And screening's logic — testing
    healthy people for probabilities — scales the counseling burden
    society-wide. The technology's curve is exponential; the ethics stack is
    where each increment lands.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Human Genome Project (Nature 2001); UK Biobank publications; NCGEN guidelines; Casgevy approval (2023) for CRISPR therapy.</p>
</TopicPage>
